@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.1893 = private unnamed_addr constant [922 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `sys.version[:3]`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIf the current major or minor version consists of multiple digits,\0A`sys.version[:3]` will truncate the version number (e.g., `\223.10\22` would\0Abecome `\223.1\22`). This is likely unintended, and can lead to subtle bugs if\0Athe version string is used to test against a specific Python version.\0A\0AInstead, use `sys.version_info` to access the current major and minor\0Aversion numbers as a tuple, which can be compared to other tuples\0Awithout issue.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Asys.version[:3]  # Evaluates to \223.1\22 on Python 3.10.\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Asys.version_info[:2]  # Evaluates to (3, 10) on Python 3.10.\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `sys.version`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.version)\0A- [Python documentation: `sys.version_info`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.version_info)\0A", align 1
@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.1894 = private unnamed_addr constant [65 x i8] c"`sys.version[:3]` referenced (python3.10), use `sys.version_info`", align 1
