@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.1896 = private unnamed_addr constant [865 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `sys.version[2]`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIf the current major or minor version consists of multiple digits,\0A`sys.version[2]` will select the first digit of the minor number only\0A(e.g., `\223.10\22` would evaluate to `\221\22`). This is likely unintended, and\0Acan lead to subtle bugs if the version is used to test against a minor\0Aversion number.\0A\0AInstead, use `sys.version_info.minor` to access the current minor version\0Anumber.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Asys.version[2]  # Evaluates to \221\22 on Python 3.10.\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Af\22{sys.version_info.minor}\22  # Evaluates to \2210\22 on Python 3.10.\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `sys.version`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.version)\0A- [Python documentation: `sys.version_info`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.version_info)\0A", align 1
