@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.1902 = private unnamed_addr constant [922 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `sys.version[:1]`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIf the major version number consists of more than one digit, this will\0Aselect the first digit of the major version number only (e.g., `\2210.0\22`\0Awould evaluate to `\221\22`). This is likely unintended, and can lead to subtle\0Abugs in future versions of Python if the version string is used to test\0Aagainst a specific major version number.\0A\0AInstead, use `sys.version_info.major` to access the current major version\0Anumber.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Asys.version[:1]  # If using Python 10, this evaluates to \221\22.\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Af\22{sys.version_info.major}\22  # If using Python 10, this evaluates to \2210\22.\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `sys.version`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.version)\0A- [Python documentation: `sys.version_info`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.version_info)\0A", align 1
