@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.1908 = private unnamed_addr constant [930 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of Django's `extra` function where one or more arguments\0Apassed are not literal expressions.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ADjango's `extra` function can be used to execute arbitrary SQL queries,\0Awhich can in turn lead to SQL injection vulnerabilities.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom django.contrib.auth.models import User\0A\0A# String interpolation creates a security loophole that could be used\0A# for SQL injection:\0AUser.objects.all().extra(select={\22test\22: \22%secure\22 % \22nos\22})\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom django.contrib.auth.models import User\0A\0A# SQL injection is impossible if all arguments are literal expressions:\0AUser.objects.all().extra(select={\22test\22: \22secure\22})\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Django documentation: SQL injection protection](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/topics/security/#sql-injection-protection)\0A- [Common Weakness Enumeration: CWE-89](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)\0A", align 1
