@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.2169 = private unnamed_addr constant [1442 x i8] c"## What it does\0ACheck for uses of `.nunique()` to check if a Pandas Series is constant\0A(i.e., contains only one unique value).\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`.nunique()` is computationally inefficient for checking if a Series is\0Aconstant.\0A\0AConsider, for example, a Series of length `n` that consists of increasing\0Ainteger values (e.g., 1, 2, 3, 4). The `.nunique()` method will iterate\0Aover the entire Series to count the number of unique values. But in this\0Acase, we can detect that the Series is non-constant after visiting the\0Afirst two values, which are non-equal.\0A\0AIn general, `.nunique()` requires iterating over the entire Series, while a\0Amore efficient approach allows short-circuiting the operation as soon as a\0Anon-equal value is found.\0A\0AInstead of calling `.nunique()`, convert the Series to a NumPy array, and\0Acheck if all values in the array are equal to the first observed value.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport pandas as pd\0A\0Adata = pd.Series(range(1000))\0Aif data.nunique() <= 1:\0A    print(\22Series is constant\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport pandas as pd\0A\0Adata = pd.Series(range(1000))\0Aarray = data.to_numpy()\0Aif array.shape[0] == 0 or (array[0] == array).all():\0A    print(\22Series is constant\22)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Pandas Cookbook: \22Constant Series\22](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/cookbook.html#constant-series)\0A- [Pandas documentation: `nunique`](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/reference/api/pandas.Series.nunique.html)\0A", align 1
