@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.2185 = private unnamed_addr constant [960 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for equality comparisons to boolean literals.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A[PEP 8] recommends against using the equality operators `==` and `!=` to\0Acompare values to `True` or `False`.\0A\0AInstead, use `if cond:` or `if not cond:` to check for truth values.\0A\0AIf you intend to check if a value is the boolean literal `True` or `False`,\0Aconsider using `is` or `is not` to check for identity instead.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aif foo == True:\0A    ...\0A\0Aif bar == False:\0A    ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aif foo:\0A    ...\0A\0Aif not bar:\0A    ...\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0A\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as it may alter runtime behavior when\0Aused with libraries that override the `==`/`__eq__` or `!=`/`__ne__` operators.\0AIn these cases, `is`/`is not` may not be equivalent to `==`/`!=`. For more\0Ainformation, see [this issue].\0A\0A[PEP 8]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/#programming-recommendations\0A[this issue]: https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/issues/4560\0A", align 1
