@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.2202 = private unnamed_addr constant [900 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for object type comparisons using `==` and other comparison\0Aoperators.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AUnlike a direct type comparison, `isinstance` will also check if an object\0Ais an instance of a class or a subclass thereof.\0A\0AIf you want to check for an exact type match, use `is` or `is not`.\0A\0A## Known problems\0AWhen using libraries that override the `==` (`__eq__`) operator (such as NumPy,\0APandas, and SQLAlchemy), this rule may produce false positives, as converting\0Afrom `==` to `is` or `is not` will change the behavior of the code.\0A\0AFor example, the following operations are _not_ equivalent:\0A```python\0Aimport numpy as np\0A\0Anp.array([True, False]) == False\0A# array([False,  True])\0A\0Anp.array([True, False]) is False\0A# False\0A```\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aif type(obj) == type(1):\0A    pass\0A\0Aif type(obj) == int:\0A    pass\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aif isinstance(obj, int):\0A    pass\0A```\0A", align 1
