@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.2250 = private unnamed_addr constant [1035 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for unnecessary positional indices in format strings.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn Python 3.1 and later, format strings can use implicit positional\0Areferences. For example, `\22{0}, {1}\22.format(\22Hello\22, \22World\22)` can be\0Arewritten as `\22{}, {}\22.format(\22Hello\22, \22World\22)`.\0A\0AIf the positional indices appear exactly in-order, they can be omitted\0Ain favor of automatic indices to improve readability.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0A\22{0}, {1}\22.format(\22Hello\22, \22World\22)  # \22Hello, World\22\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0A\22{}, {}\22.format(\22Hello\22, \22World\22)  # \22Hello, World\22\0A```\0A\0AThis fix is marked as unsafe because:\0A- Comments attached to arguments are not moved, which can cause comments to mismatch the actual arguments.\0A- If arguments have side effects (e.g., print), reordering may change program behavior.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Format String Syntax](https://docs.python.org/3/library/string.html#format-string-syntax)\0A- [Python documentation: `str.format`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#str.format)\0A", align 1
